+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+`src/index.ts` has `resolvePointer` with a private `parsePointer` implementing RFC 6901 §3/§4 syntax. JSON Patch needs the same pointer parsing plus parent/last-token addressing, so `parsePointer` is reused. Inputs are untrusted JSON, so `__proto__` and similar keys must be handled safely and callers' values never mutated.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+- Exact RFC 6902 §4 semantics for all six operations, atomic per §5.
+- No mutation of `document` or `patch`; result shares no mutable structure with the inputs.
+- Safe with hostile keys (`__proto__`, `constructor`).
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+- Diffing, merge patch, in-place mode, performance tuning for huge documents.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **Work on a deep clone.** The document is deep-cloned once, operations mutate the clone, and the clone is returned. If any operation throws, the clone is discarded, giving RFC 6902 §5 atomicity. Values taken from the patch (`add`/`replace` `value`) are cloned on insertion, so the result never aliases the patch; `copy` clones the source value. Cloning uses own enumerable properties and builds objects with `Object.defineProperty`, so an own `__proto__` key stays an ordinary member and never changes a prototype. Every assignment into an object uses the same helper.
+- **Error type.** A new `JsonPatchError` is thrown for every failure, including pointer syntax errors (the `JsonPointerError` message is wrapped), so callers catch one class.
+- **Patch validation (§3, §4).** `patch` must be an array; each operation a non-null, non-array object with string `op` among the six and string `path`. `add`, `replace`, `test` require a `value` member (own property; `null` is valid, absence is not). `move`, `copy` require a string `from`. Unknown extra members are ignored, as §4 says. Validation of an operation happens when it is applied; since failure discards everything, the outcome is the same as validating up front.
+- **add (§4.1).** Empty path replaces the whole document. Otherwise the parent must exist and be a container: for objects the member is set (replacing any existing one); for arrays the token is `-` (append) or an index in `0..length` (insert, shifting later elements); anything else, including leading zeros, negatives or index > length, is an error.
+- **remove (§4.2) / replace (§4.3).** Target must exist (own member, or array index < length). `-` never refers to an existing element, so it is an error. `replace` with an empty path replaces the document. `remove` with an empty path is an error: RFC 6902 does not define a document with no value, and JSON `undefined` is not a JSON value. This is a documented choice where the RFC is silent.
+- **move (§4.4).** `from` must exist; it is an error if `from` is a proper prefix of `path` (token-wise, so `/a` is not a prefix of `/ab`; moving a value into its own child). `from` equal to `path` is a successful no-op (after checking `from` exists). Otherwise remove at `from`, then add at `path`, so array indices in `path` are interpreted after removal, per the RFC.
+- **copy (§4.5).** `from` must exist; the value is deep-cloned then added at `path`.
+- **test (§4.6).** Deep equality by JSON value: same type; numbers by numeric value (so `1` and `1.0` are equal); strings code-unit equal; arrays same length and order; objects same set of member names regardless of order. Failure throws. Missing target is a failure. Empty path tests the whole document.
+- **Traversal safety.** Object lookup uses own-property checks, as `resolvePointer` does; arrays only accept canonical indices.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- Cloning the document costs O(size) per call; accepted for the no-mutation guarantee.
+- Rejecting `remove` of the root could differ from other libraries that return `null`; recorded as a deliberate choice, easy to revisit.
+- Non-JSON inputs (functions, `undefined`, cycles) are not supported; cycles would overflow the stack.
