+# Proposal
+
+## Why
+
+Services need to change stored JSON documents and API payloads with a standard patch format instead of ad-hoc code. The library already resolves JSON Pointers (RFC 6901), which is the addressing mechanism JSON Patch is built on.
+
+## What Changes
+
+- Add `applyPatch(document, patch)` exported from `src/index.ts`, implementing JSON Patch (RFC 6902) with the six operations `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy` and `test`.
+- It returns the patched document as a new value, never mutating the document or the patch, and throws a `JsonPatchError` when the patch is invalid or an operation fails (RFC 6902 §5: the whole patch is then not applied).
+- Add `JsonPatchError`, exported alongside it.
+- Governing standards: RFC 6902 (§3 document structure, §4 operations, §5 error handling), RFC 6901 for pointers (§3, §4, §7 for `-`).
+- No breaking changes: only new exports; `resolvePointer` and `JsonPointerError` are untouched.
+- Out of scope: JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7396), generating a patch by diffing two documents, in-place application, URI fragment pointers (RFC 6901 §6), the `application/json-patch+json` media type, and any runtime dependency.
+
+## Capabilities
+
+### New Capabilities
+- `json-patch`: applying an RFC 6902 patch to a JSON document without mutating inputs, including validation and error behaviour.
+
+### Modified Capabilities
+
+## Impact
+
+- `src/index.ts` (new exports), `src/index.test.ts` (tests). No dependency or `package.json` change.
